\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Three of four metrics excluding crossing rate (precision, inverse FPR, and additive composite) consistently select deep layers: L60 (94\%) and L63 (98\%). Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects middle layers (L30 and L47), consistent with the AUROC pattern discussed in \cref{sec:discussion}. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all four models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Biased}$^a$ & \textbf{Corrected}$^b$ & $p$ \textbf{(corr.)} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 (44\%) & L14 (44\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
OT-7B & L0 (0\%) & L16 (57\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
QwQ-32B & L2 (3\%) & L60 (94\%) & ${<}0.005$ \\
R1-32B & L10 (16\%) & L63 (98\%) & ${<}0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Layer selection sensitivity. $^a$\,bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate. $^b$\,Consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite). Rightmost: HS vs.\ text precision $p$-value at corrected layer (e.g., QwQ-32B biased-layer: bal\_acc $p{=}0.35$, precision $p{=}0.83$, both non-significant).}
\label{tab:layer_selection}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_layer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer metric heatmap (2$\times$2). Each column: one of five metrics (min-max normalized). Red dashed: biased layer (bal\_acc $\times$ crossing\_rate); green dashed: corrected layer (consensus of threshold-dependent metrics). For R1-8B, both coincide at L14. At 32B, biased selections cluster near input layers while threshold-dependent metrics peak at deep layers.}
\label{fig:layer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

The downstream impact is severe: at biased layers, only the two 7--8B models show significant HS advantage; both 32B models fail. At corrected layers, all four models pass Holm-Bonferroni correction (\cref{sec:evaluation}). Had we reported only biased-layer results, we would have concluded a scale-dependent HS advantage that is entirely a methodological artifact.

\subsection{HS vs.\ Text Comparison}\label{sec:hs-text}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llclrrlrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Enc.} & $W$ & $\Delta$\textbf{BA} & $\Delta$\textbf{Prec} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Delta$\textbf{FPR} & \textbf{HB} \\
\midrule
R1-8B & L14 & 384d & 15 & +8.2 & \textbf{+32.5} & [+20.2, +45.2] & $-$7.4 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +5.8 & [$-$7.4, +19.5] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
OT-7B & L16 & 384d & 3 & +10.7 & +21.9 & [+14.4, +29.6] & $-$17.1 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +18.1 & [+8.4, +29.0] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \checkmark \\[2pt]
QwQ & L60 & 384d & 3 & +15.4 & +23.6 & [+12.9, +35.1] & $-$9.8 & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +9.4 & [$-$7.1, +27.7] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\[2pt]
R1-32B & L63 & 384d & 1 & \textbf{+18.7} & +15.4 & [+8.7, +22.6] & \textbf{$-$14.6} & \checkmark \\
 & & 1024d & & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & +11.7 & [$-$0.2, +24.3] & \multicolumn{1}{c}{--} & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{HS vs.\ text at corrected layers (LR probes, 60/20/20 split). Enc.: text encoder (384d = MiniLM-L6-v2, 1024d = BGE-large). $\Delta$: HS$-$text difference (pp). 95\% CI: bootstrap interval for $\Delta$Prec (10{,}000 resamples). HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$); all four 384d model-level tests pass. 1024d: HB over 4 model-level precision tests. Values from canonical split; 5-seed mean$\pm$std in \cref{app:multi_seed}.$^*$\,R1-32B: precision std${=}0.128$, FPR std${=}0.145$ across 5 seeds (\cref{app:multi_seed}). BGE $\Delta$BA/$\Delta$FPR in \cref{app:encoder_robustness}.}
\label{tab:hs_vs_text}
\end{table}

Against the 384d baseline, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all four models across all three metrics (\cref{tab:hs_vs_text}), though R1-8B FPR significance is fragile across seeds (2/5 uncorrected, 1/5 Bonferroni-corrected; \cref{tab:r18b_fpr_seeds}). At 7--8B, the evidence is strong: precision gains of +32.5pp (R1-8B) and +21.9pp (OT-7B), stable across seeds (std ${<}4$pp). R1-8B FPR advantage is directionally consistent across seeds (5/5) but not robust to data split (2/5 reach $p{<}0.05$ uncorrected; after Bonferroni correction for 5 seeds, 1/5 remains significant; \cref{app:multi_seed}). At 32B, both models are significant (HB corrected) but with model-specific caveats: R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces), and QwQ-32B's position baseline is competitive (\cref{sec:32b-analysis}). R1-32B passes 5/5 seeds (paired $\Delta$Prec std${=}2.0$pp, despite absolute precision std${=}12.8$pp) but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}). Overall, 18/20 model-seed combinations pass full HB; R1-8B FPR instability accounts for both failures (\cref{app:multi_seed}). Control task selectivity confirms genuine safety signal: 12.5--19.7pp above chance (\cref{app:control}).

\paragraph{Capacity control.} PCA reduction of HS features to 384d (matching text encoder dimensionality, 79--92\% variance retained) preserves the precision advantage on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all $p{<}0.012$), with all four model-level precision tests passing Holm-Bonferroni. Two individual metric comparisons are non-significant: R1-8B balanced accuracy (+1.3pp, $p{=}0.731$, effect too small at $n{=}43$) and QwQ-32B FPR ($-$11.4pp, $p{=}0.038$, below HB threshold). PCA retains directions of maximum variance in hidden-state space, which need not align with the semantic dimensions captured by a trained text encoder; the control rules out a pure capacity explanation but not all encoder-quality effects. This confirms a modality effect rather than a capacity artifact.

\paragraph{Encoder robustness.} The 1024d rows in \cref{tab:hs_vs_text} replace MiniLM (384d) with BGE-large~\citep{xiao2023cpack} (1024d, a 2.7$\times$ stronger encoder). The HS precision advantage persists directionally on all four models but narrows substantially: only OT-7B survives Holm-Bonferroni (1/4 precision tests). HS balanced accuracy advantage is also directionally positive on all four models (+8.4 to +10.9pp), with 2/4 (OT-7B, R1-32B) significant under Holm-Bonferroni; FPR advantage is significant only for OT-7B (1/4). R1-8B shows an adverse FPR direction (HS FPR 1.2pp higher than BGE, $p{=}0.564$). The advantage is encoder-conditional rather than absolute (\cref{fig:forest_precision}). Layer selection (Contribution 1) is encoder-independent, as it concerns hidden-state properties across layers rather than the text baseline.

\paragraph{Position mediation.} Position ($t/T$) accounts for 9--20pp of balanced accuracy depending on model and window size,\footnote{R1-32B's 20.4pp drop reflects $W{=}1$ (no temporal context); at $W{\geq}3$ with temporal windowing, drops are 9--17pp.} but all models retain non-positional signal above chance (all $p{<}0.001$; \cref{app:position_residual}). Three of four models retain significant non-positional HS advantage over text (residualized gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB corrected); R1-32B's gap is +1.9pp ($p{=}0.914$, $n{=}20$), consistent with a position-mediated advantage. Temporal windowing reduces position dependence (R1-8B: 24.3pp drop at $W{=}3$ vs.\ 16.5pp at $W{=}15$).

\paragraph{Caveats.} R1-32B achieves significance only at $W{=}1$ with 25\% coverage (5/20 traces); at $W{\geq}3$, dimensionality ($51200 \times W$ for $n{=}20$) prevents significance. Sweeping regularization $C$ over $[0.01, 100]$ yields ${<}2$pp variation in balanced accuracy; the HS advantage is also stable across decision thresholds (BA variation ${<}1.5$pp across $\tau \in [0.3, 0.7]$; \cref{app:threshold_sensitivity}). QwQ-32B HS lead time is negative ($-$1.2 to +1.9 steps vs.\ text 3.7--12.3 steps): higher precision at later detection. On benign traces, probes produce 39--52\% false alarm rates, so current methods characterize the commitment phenomenon rather than constitute production-ready detectors. Per-model details in \cref{sec:32b-analysis}.

\subsection{32B Model-Specific Analysis}\label{sec:32b-analysis}

R1-32B and QwQ-32B share the Qwen-32B architecture (64 layers, 5120 hidden) but present opposite detection profiles (\cref{tab:32b_divergence}); training method is the likely differentiator.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Cov.} & \textbf{Lead (steps)} & \textbf{HB} & \textbf{Pos.\ margin} \\
\midrule
R1-32B & 25\% & +11.7 to +13.3 & \checkmark{} ($W{=}1$) & +14.2pp \\
QwQ-32B & 74\% & $-$1.2 to +1.9 & \checkmark{} (all $W$) & $-$3.6pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{32B cross-model divergence. R1-32B provides limited early detection with narrow coverage; QwQ-32B provides robust significance with poor lead time. HB: \checkmark{} = model-level test passes Holm-Bonferroni ($\alpha{=}0.05$). Pos.\ margin: HS balanced accuracy minus position-only baseline.}
\label{tab:32b_divergence}
\end{table}

R1-32B detects commitment 11--13 steps early with a +14.2pp margin over the position baseline, which confirms genuine representational signal, but covers only 25\% of traces at the single significant window ($W{=}1$). QwQ-32B achieves statistical significance at all window sizes with 74\% coverage, but HS probes fire later than text probes (negative lead time). Consistent with the universal position analysis (\cref{sec:hs-text}), QwQ-32B's position baseline achieves higher balanced accuracy (0.832 vs.\ 0.796). HS precision (+4.5pp, $p{=}0.133$) and FPR (+2.5pp, $p{=}0.279$) margins over the position baseline are directionally positive but not statistically significant at $n{=}19$. The divergence implies that per-model validation is essential: no single detection configuration generalizes even within the same architecture at the same scale, and a system validated on one 32B model would fail on the other.

\subsection{Temporal Context}\label{sec:temporal}

Temporal windowing ($W{>}1$) improves both modalities on all four models (\cref{fig:window_ablation}). Text precision improves +8 to +23pp from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$; HS precision improves +1.7 to +12.9pp. At 7--8B, the modality advantage (+22 to +33pp precision) exceeds the temporal improvement (+13pp). Both effects are complementary: temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) combined with principled layer selection yields the most consistent gains. Full per-model temporal ablations appear in \cref{app:temporal_details}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_forest_precision.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of HS precision advantage over text probes. Blue: vs.\ MiniLM-384d; orange: vs.\ BGE-1024d. Solid markers: survives Holm-Bonferroni; hollow: does not. All four 384d comparisons are significant; against BGE-1024d, only OT-7B survives correction.}
\label{fig:forest_precision}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Negative Results}\label{sec:negative}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{p{1.5cm}p{2.9cm}p{2.3cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Result} & \textbf{Limitation} \\
\midrule
BOCPD & 0\% det.\ on 3/4 models & Commitment gradual \\
CUSUM & $<$ threshold, all models & Same as BOCPD \\
Mahalanobis & $\Delta d \approx 0$ & Signal in minor PCs \\
RepE & QwQ 0.756; R1-32B 0.559$^a$ & Model-dependent \\
MLP & 3/4 worse than LR & Overfitting \\
CCS & No gain over supervised$^b$ & Alignment mismatch \\
Transfer & Best 59.5\% (PCA)$^c$ & Model-specific repr. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Seven alternative approaches. $^a$\,RepE QwQ approaches supervised bal\_acc but precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700. $^b$\,Semi-supervised (requires CP labels). $^c$\,Best direct transfer 67.3\% but 1\% crossing rate.}
\label{tab:negative}
\end{table}

No alternative method consistently matches supervised probing across all four models (\cref{tab:negative}). These failures are themselves informative: they reveal that safety commitment representations are gradual (ruling out change-point methods), concentrated in minor principal components (ruling out distance-based methods), and model-specific (ruling out universal direction approaches). We prioritize precision because in a hypothetical deployment scenario, false positives on pre-CP steps would waste intervention resources, while missed post-CP steps merely reduce coverage.

\paragraph{Change-point methods.} BOCPD~\citep{adams2007bayesian} achieves 0\% detection on 3/4 models because safety commitment is gradual rather than abrupt: the probe score sequence transitions smoothly over 5--15 steps without the discontinuity that BOCPD's hazard function requires. On R1-8B (the sole model with non-zero detection), the best BOCPD configuration ($\lambda{=}10$) achieves 93\% detection rate but with lower lead time (+3.5 steps) than simple thresholding (+4.9 steps). CUSUM similarly fails because cumulative evidence never exceeds the decision boundary on smooth transitions. The gradual nature of commitment is itself an empirical finding: it rules out the entire class of abrupt-change detectors and motivates the supervised probe approach that explicitly learns the transition boundary.

\paragraph{RepE inconsistency.} RepE computes the class-conditioned mean difference as a fixed direction (analytic), while LR optimizes cross-entropy to learn the classification boundary (iterative). Both require the same labeled data; the key distinction is parameterization, not supervision level. This difference is consequential: RepE mean-difference directions~\citep{zou2023representation} illustrate the metric-dependent nature of method comparison. On QwQ-32B, RepE achieves 0.756 balanced accuracy (comparable to supervised LR at 0.778), but its precision is 0.512 vs.\ LR 0.700 and its FPR is 0.309 vs.\ 0.120. The mean-difference direction captures enough signal to classify steps above chance, but fires indiscriminately on pre-CP steps, producing 2.6$\times$ more false alarms than supervised probes. On R1-32B, RepE nearly fails entirely (0.559 balanced accuracy). The inconsistency across two models sharing the same architecture confirms that a single linear direction computed from class means does not reliably capture safety commitment structure.

Cross-model transfer produces no practical utility across 17 configurations; stronger alignment methods (CCA, model stitching~\cite{kornblith2019cka,bansal2021stitching}) remain unexplored. Detailed per-method analysis in \cref{app:negative_details}.

