\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Layer selection is an underexamined methodological choice in the probing literature that can silently determine a study's conclusions. Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) demonstrates that a single metric component, the crossing rate, reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, all four models are significant (\cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which pushes a linear probe to the noise floor and inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. Any composite metric that incorporates crossing rate is susceptible to this bias, and we hypothesize the effect generalizes beyond safety probing to any threshold-based detection study. We recommend that probing studies report results under at least two complementary layer selection criteria and that multi-metric ablations become standard practice.

\paragraph{Temporal context and modality choice.}
Both temporal context and modality choice improve detection, but their relative magnitudes differ by scale: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement (\cref{sec:temporal}); at 32B, relative magnitudes are model-dependent. Temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent improvement regardless of modality, while modality choice requires principled layer selection.

\paragraph{Encoder-conditional advantage.}
Part of the observed advantage may reflect text encoder quality rather than hidden-state informational superiority. At matched 384d dimensionality via PCA (79--92\% variance retained), the precision advantage persists on all four models (+16.8 to +23.1pp, all four model-level precision tests pass Holm-Bonferroni), eliminating capacity gap as the sole explanation. A stronger 1024d encoder closes most of the significance gap (1/4 HB-significant), suggesting a continuum rather than a binary modality distinction. This pattern predicts that LLM-based text encoders (e.g., E5-Mistral-7B, 4096d) would further narrow or close the gap, bounding the practical lifetime of any HS advantage claim as text encoding improves.

\paragraph{Per-model evidence and design implications.}
Robustness splits by model, not scale: OT-7B and QwQ-32B (5/5 seeds HB each) are most stable, R1-8B FPR is seed-fragile (3/5 HB), and R1-32B passes all seeds but its advantage is position-mediated (residualized gap NS; \cref{app:position_residual}). Distillation and native reasoning training produce qualitatively different safety representation geometry within the same architecture. If future work achieves acceptable false positive rates, temporal windowing ($W{=}3$--$15$) with principled layer selection provides a reasonable starting range. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage) provide converging evidence of genuine safety representations; direct erasure methods (LEACE; \citealp{belrose2023leace}) remain untested.

